@startuml
'=============================================================================
' KIND      MEASURED -- processes and stores named from the code that
'           implements them; each store is a real path under
'           fleet/workers/<name>/.
' GROUNDING fleet/inbox.py (poll, _poll_contract_item, LEDGER="ledger.jsonl",
'           FOLDERS, retained_name); fleet/input_set.py (SET_FILE, bind,
'           _materialize, fingerprint, run_input); fleet/fleet.py
'           (record_run, load); adapters/xlsx.py; worker/runtime.py;
'           fleet/system_map.py; supervisor/app.py.
'
' NOTATION  Data Flow Diagram, level 1. Circles/rounded boxes are
'           PROCESSES, the open-ended boxes are DATA STORES, the actors
'           are EXTERNAL ENTITIES. Arrows are DATA, never control flow --
'           see docs/architecture/modelling-guide.md §2.
'
' No LLM appears anywhere in this diagram. That is the point of it: once
' work is established, the recurring path runs without one.
'=============================================================================
!theme plain
title Learning Lab — established operation, data flow (DFD level 1, MEASURED)

skinparam componentStyle rectangle

actor "Operator" as OP
actor "Company\ndata files" as SRC

component "1.0\nRead + identify\narriving item" as P1
component "2.0\nBind role /\ncomplete input set" as P2
component "3.0\nValidate against\ninput contract" as P3
component "4.0\nExecute the\ntask model" as P4
component "5.0\nRecord run,\nfile the item" as P5
component "6.0\nDerive the\nsystem map" as P6
component "7.0\nInterpret +\npropose" as P7

database "D1  worker.json\n     versions/vN.json" as D1
database "D2  input_contracts/vN.json" as D2
database "D3  input_set.json" as D3
database "D4  ledger.jsonl" as D4
database "D5  runs.jsonl" as D5
database "D6  processed/  exceptions/" as D6
database "D7  assessment, backlog,\n     improvements, rulebook" as D7

SRC --> P1 : file dropped into inbox/
P1 --> D4 : item_id (content digest),\nclaim + precondition
D4 --> P1 : last recorded state\n(duplicate? staged?)
P1 --> P2 : item + declared roles

P2 --> D3 : binding, materialized member
D3 --> P2 : open set, completeness
D1 --> P2 : source_roles, slot kinds
P2 --> P3 : complete input set + fingerprint

D2 --> P3 : sheet / collection / header_row\nfor this version
P3 --> P5 : refusal (named reason)
P3 --> P4 : validated rows

D1 --> P4 : the established model version
P4 --> P5 : result / refusal / applied effect

P5 --> D5 : run record, version, run_input.fingerprint
P5 --> D4 : terminal state
P5 --> D6 : raw arrival retained by digest

D1 --> P6 : established workers, declared\ninputs, destinations
D5 --> P6 : run and exception state
P6 --> OP : read-only company system map

D4 --> P7 : exceptions
D5 --> P7 : run history
D7 --> P7 : prior findings, rules
P7 --> D7 : findings, proposals\n(never activation)
P7 --> OP : interpretation, grounded in referents
OP --> D7 : human-gated activation

note as N1
**Boundaries this flow keeps**

* Every store written by 5.0 is written AFTER the claim in D4, so an
  interrupted pass is reconstructable rather than invisible.
* 3.0 emits a NAMED refusal into the same path as a result. A refusal
  is an outcome, not an error to be retried away.
* `run_input.fingerprint` in D5 is what makes run-exactly-once
  decidable across a crash: recovery matches on it rather than
  guessing from file position.
* 7.0 writes only to D7, and only proposals. The arrow that turns a
  proposal into an active rule starts at the Operator, not at 7.0.
end note
@enduml
